   <caption unique="1091449">Instruction Operand Encoding</caption>
   </table>
  </operand_table>
 <longdesc><para unique="1091452">Pushes the contents of the <indexterm><primary>General-purpose registers:pushing all</primary></indexterm>general-purpose registers onto the stack. The registers are stored on the stack in the following order: EAX, ECX, EDX, EBX, ESP (original value), EBP, ESI, and EDI (if the current operand-size attribute is 32) and AX, CX, DX, BX, SP (original value), BP, SI, and DI (if the operand-size attribute is 16). These instruc&#173;tions perform the reverse operation of the POPA/POPAD instructions. The value pushed for the ESP or SP register is its value before prior to pushing the first register (see the &#8220;Operation&#8221; section below).</para>
  <para unique="1091453">The PUSHA (push all) and PUSHAD (push all double) mnemonics reference the same opcode. The PUSHA instruc&#173;tion is intended for use when the operand-size attribute is 16 and the PUSHAD instruction for when the operand-size attribute is 32. Some assemblers may force the operand size to 16 when PUSHA is used and to 32 when PUSHAD is used. Others may treat these mnemonics as synonyms (PUSHA/PUSHAD) and use the current setting of the operand-size attribute to determine the size of values to be pushed from the stack, regardless of the mnemonic used.</para>
  <para unique="1091454">In the real-address mode, if the ESP or SP register is 1, 3, or 5 when PUSHA/PUSHAD executes: an #SS exception is generated but not delivered (the stack error reported prevents #SS delivery). Next, the processor generates a #DF exception and enters a shutdown state as described in the #DF discussion in <link unique="1091456" linkend="&lt;c&gt;__sdm_vol3A_ch07.fm#26613: H1Heading: Chapter 5 Protected-Mode Exceptions and Interrupts" xrefstyle="ChapterNum">Chapter 7</link> of the Intel&#174; 64 and IA&#8209;32 Architectures Software Developer&#8217;s Manual, Volume 3A.</para>
  <para unique="1091458">This instruction executes as described in compatibility mode and legacy mode. It is not valid in 64-bit mode.</para>
  </longdesc>
 <operation>
  <programlisting>IF 64-bit Mode
	THEN #UD
FI;
IF OperandSize = 32 (* PUSHAD instruction *)
	THEN
		Temp := (ESP);
		Push(EAX);
		Push(ECX);
		Push(EDX);
		Push(EBX);
		Push(Temp);
		Push(EBP);
		Push(ESI);
		Push(EDI);
	ELSE (* OperandSize = 16, PUSHA instruction *)
		Temp := (SP);
		Push(AX);
		Push(CX);
		Push(DX);
		Push(BX);
		Push(Temp);
		Push(BP);
		Push(SI);
		Push(DI);
FI;</programlisting>
  </operation>
 <subsection title="Flags Affected">
  <para unique="1091486">None.</para>
  </subsection>
 <subsection title="Protected Mode Exceptions">
  <variablelist>
   <variablelistentry>
    <term>#SS(0)</term>
    <listitem><para unique="1091488">If the starting or ending stack address is outside the stack segment limit.</para>
     </listitem>
    </variablelistentry>
   <variablelistentry>
    <term>#PF(fault-code)</term>
    <listitem><para unique="1091489">If a page fault occurs.</para>
     </listitem>
    </variablelistentry>
   <variablelistentry>
    <term>#AC(0)</term>
    <listitem><para unique="1091490">If an unaligned memory reference is made while the current privilege level is 3 and alignment checking is enabled.</para>
     </listitem>
    </variablelistentry>
   <variablelistentry>
    <term>#UD </term>
    <listitem><para unique="1091491">If the LOCK prefix is used.</para>
     </listitem>
    </variablelistentry>
   </variablelist>
  </subsection>
 <subsection title="Real-Address Mode Exceptions">
  <variablelist>
   <variablelistentry>
    <term>#GP</term>
    <listitem><para unique="1091493">If the ESP or SP register contains 7, 9, 11, 13, or 15.</para>
     </listitem>
    </variablelistentry>
   <variablelistentry>
    <term>#UD </term>
    <listitem><para unique="1091494">If the LOCK prefix is used.</para>
     </listitem>
    </variablelistentry>
   </variablelist>
  </subsection>
 <subsection title="Virtual-8086 Mode Exceptions">
  <variablelist>
   <variablelistentry>
    <term>#GP(0)</term>
    <listitem><para unique="1091496">If the ESP or SP register contains 7, 9, 11, 13, or 15.</para>
     </listitem>
    </variablelistentry>
   <variablelistentry>
    <term>#PF(fault-code)</term>
    <listitem><para unique="1091497">If a page fault occurs.</para>
     </listitem>
    </variablelistentry>
   <variablelistentry>
    <term>#AC(0)</term>
    <listitem><para unique="1091498">If an unaligned memory reference is made while alignment checking is enabled.</para>
     </listitem>
    </variablelistentry>
   <variablelistentry>
    <term>#UD </term>
    <listitem><para unique="1091499">If the LOCK prefix is used.</para>
     </listitem>
    </variablelistentry>
   </variablelist>
  </subsection>
 <subsection title="Compatibility Mode Exceptions">
  <para unique="1091501" format="xIndent1">Same exceptions as in protected mode.</para>
  </subsection>
 <subsection title="64-Bit Mode Exceptions">
  <variablelist>
   <variablelistentry>
    <term>#UD</term>
    <listitem><para unique="1091503">If in 64-bit mode.</para>
     </listitem>
    </variablelistentry>
   </variablelist>
  </subsection>
 </instruction>
